  "cause": "**The United States published an accounting of other people's oil money on Thursday, and redacted the part that would identify who received it.** The **State Department**'s 53-page report covers flows into and out of US-controlled accounts between **3 January and 30 September 2026**: **\\$12.26bn** received from *\"verified and approved entities engaging in authorized activities\"* in Venezuela — approximately **99 per cent** of it from crude oil and petroleum products, the remainder from gold, minerals and fertilisers — and more than **\\$12bn** disbursed by 30 September, leaving about **\\$169m** in the account. The funds come from about **20 depositors whose identities are redacted**. Nearly all of the disbursed money went to Venezuelan banks for **foreign-exchange interventions** — selling dollars to soak up bolívars and prop up the currency — with roughly **\\$9.4bn** to *\"legal entities\"* and **\\$2.9bn** to *\"individuals\"*, and no detail on the final recipients. **KPMG**, a Washington-based firm contracted by the State Department, is auditing the transactions. **The architecture behind the report is a seizure, and the report is its bookkeeping.** US forces raided **Caracas** in January, captured **Nicolás Maduro** and his wife and brought them to New York to face charges of drug trafficking and torture; then-vice-president **Delcy Rodríguez** took over and made agreements that gave Washington a direct stake in the proceeds of Venezuelan oil and minerals, formalised through an executive order establishing the special accounts — first at **Qatar National Bank**, then at the **Treasury**. In **August** Trump announced a deal covering **17 strategic fields** with proven potential of **65 billion barrels**, a **100-year concession**, a joint venture in which the US government retains **55 per cent**, and claimed investment of **\\$100bn**; the official text has never been published, and analysts questioned both the arithmetic and whether it addresses the obstacles that have deterred investment for two decades. The *New York Times* reported on Saturday what this has done to a national idea: generations of Venezuelans grew up believing the oil beneath them was theirs and cheap petrol a birthright, and *\"now the U.S. government is taking control of a huge portion of Venezuela's oil\"* — with production already collapsed by *\"mismanagement, political purges, corruption and sanctions.\"* Human-rights activists have warned that Washington may weight oil revenue above democratic reform; *Time* titled its assessment **\"Venezuela's Elusive Elections.\"**",
  "bullets": [
   "**A trust with redacted beneficiaries is a transfer with a receipt, and the receipt is the only part that can be audited** — \\$12.26bn in, \\$12bn out, \\$169m remaining, 20 depositors unnamed, \\$9.4bn to \"legal entities\" and \\$2.9bn to \"individuals\" whose identities the report does not give; the direct effect is that the money's route is documented and its destination is not; the compound consequence is that the one question the report was released to answer — *did this benefit Venezuelans or their captors' successors* — remains unanswerable from the document, so the disclosure converts a scandal into a format, and the next report will be judged by whether it was published rather than by what it disclosed",
   "**Foreign-exchange intervention is a subsidy whose incidence nobody is measuring** — nearly all the money returned to Venezuelan banks to sell dollars and support the bolívar; the direct effect is a stabilised currency in the short term; the compound consequence is that the benefit accrues first to whoever holds bolívars and can convert them — importers, connected firms, anyone with advance knowledge of the intervention schedule — while the cost lands on the general population through the inflation that the intervention postpones rather than prevents; an intervention without a published allocation rule is a transfer to the well-connected, whoever intends it",
   "**The auditor is hired by the disburser, which is the arrangement the report exists to correct** — KPMG contracted by the State Department to examine State Department-supervised accounts, with beneficiaries redacted in the underlying report; the direct effect is that the audit's independence is procedural rather than structural; the compound consequence is that no counterparty — not the Venezuelan National Assembly, not a multilateral institution, not Congress' own auditors — holds the matching set of books, so there is no second series to compare against, and the mathematical definition of fraud is a discrepancy between two records",
   "**Oil revenue as the metric of success displaces the political metric, and the interim authority's legitimacy gets measured in barrels** — a 100-year concession, 17 fields, \\$100bn promised, an unpublished text, and elections that remain elusive nearly a year after the capital was raided; the direct effect is that the Rodríguez government's standing with Washington tracks production and disbursement rather than any electoral calendar; the compound consequence is that the arrangement creates a constituency for delay — every party taking a share of the revenue has an interest in the transition remaining unfinished — and the promise that made the seizure defensible, a democratic transition, becomes the one deliverable with no line in the ledger",
   "**A resource committed for a century by an authority of contested legitimacy binds every government that follows** — 65 billion barrels, 100 years, 55 per cent US control of a joint venture, terms never published in either capital; the direct effect is that a future elected Venezuelan government inherits an obligation it did not negotiate and cannot easily renegotiate; the compound consequence is that the deal becomes the central issue of the election it is meant to precede — and a contract whose legitimacy is contested is worth less than the oil it covers, because every year of its life is a year of legal risk priced into the investment it was supposed to attract"
  ],
  "verse": "And do not devour your wealth amongst one another through falseness and lying and deception, and not through greed and stinginess; and do not offer your wealth for bribery, not to those without equitableness (unfair ones/ irresponsible ones/inequitable ones) and not to authority in order to be granted advantages and right while in the unright… live at all times in equitableness (fairness/responsibility) and honestly and do not dare to commit misdeeds in order thereby to increase your wealth by striving to swindle the wealth of others through unright.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 2, Verse 246",
  "hinge": "§2:246 is unusually specific about mechanism — wealth is devoured *through falseness and lying and deception*, and the instrument named is the granting of advantage *to authority* in order to be held right while in the unright. The report released on Thursday is an attempt to answer exactly that charge, and it stops one page short: it proves the money moved and declines to say to whom. The hinge is the **beneficiary register**. Publish the twenty depositors and the recipients of the \\$9.4bn and \\$2.9bn, with the legal basis for each disbursement — a list of names is the only artifact that distinguishes a trust from a slush fund, and the State Department already holds it. Publish the KPMG terms of reference and scope, and give the Venezuelan National Assembly and Congress' Government Accountability Office the same data room, so two independent parties can reconcile the series. Publish the August agreement's text before the next tranche moves. And attach a **published election calendar** to the disbursement schedule, so that the revenue which justified the intervention is conditioned on the transition it was said to serve. None of this requires returning a dollar. All of it requires naming who received one."
